{"kind":"report","text":"approved, at exact head 0c05eca5d7018f0c696c0e0df382e90649c9ccc6. Both b70c7c7c blockers are genuinely removed, the completed-flight repair is load-bearing and proven, and every gate including the full race gate is green.\n\nPRIOR BLOCKERS - both cleared, verified by grep at the head rather than by report.\n\n1. The dead Profile aliases are gone, not renamed. CheckpointProfile no longer appears anywhere in the tree. CheckpointOptions.Profile is deleted, enabled() is now exactly \"return o.Enabled\", and the false rationale - \"retained for source compatibility with older embedders\" - is gone from both structs; no occurrence of that phrasing survives. The roughly forty test call sites were migrated mechanically to CheckpointOptions{Enabled: true}, so no coverage was traded for the deletion. The single enable path is now the right one: Enabled cannot be inferred from SigningKey (a custody-less reader still consumes checkpoints) or from Pointer (optional), so one explicit switch is the minimum, not spare machinery.\n\n2. The cold-audit gate has a genuine caller again, and the restored coverage is stronger than what was dropped. TestColdAuditProgressIsSingleFlightAndPublishesAtomically drives a real cold audit (InvalidateCheckpoint then a fresh Open), holds it at a deterministic point, and asserts /v0/rebuild reports Running with exact Verified=2 and Total=records+2 rather than a monotonicity heuristic. It restores the bounded-field assertion that only running, verified and total are exposed, holds a second reader through the audit so neither returns early, requires both joined readers to receive an identical complete Durable projection, and requires the endpoint to fall quiet afterwards. SetRebuildTestGate is no longer dead: it has a real caller, and so does SetProjectionRebuildTestGate, so the two gates are now two live behaviours rather than one live and one abandoned.\n\nTHE RACE REPAIR (child assert 06c18977) - correct, minimal, and load-bearing. snapshotFlight now refuses to join an installed flight whose done channel is already closed, falling through to start a fresh one. That is the right cut: a completed flight can linger installed until its worker reacquires flightMu, and its result was computed against a head and application profile that may since have moved, so joining it could publish a projection derived under a fold contract no longer in force. I specifically checked the ABA hazard the fix could have introduced - a superseded worker clearing the newly installed flight - and it is already handled: the worker's cleanup is a guarded compare, \"if w.flight.Load() == flight\" under flightMu, which predates this change, so a stale worker leaves a newer flight alone. The cost is bounded to the microseconds a completed flight remains installed. What remains is the pre-existing and deliberate single-flight semantic - a joiner of an in-progress flight receives that flight's head, not the newest - which is honest, unchanged here, and documented as process-lifetime work.\n\nThe regression is deterministic rather than timing-dependent: the test injects a completed snapshotFlight carrying the pre-change snapshot, then changes the projection profile. Without the guard the stale flight is joined, no rebuild happens, and the folder and profile assertions fail. I verified that by mutation rather than trusting it (MR1 below).\n\nMUTATIONS at this head - five broken guards, five caught.\n- MR1 reverting the completed-flight guard to \"return flight\": CAUGHT by TestProjectionProfileChangeRebuildsFromTheSameKernelCheckpoint. The repair is load-bearing.\n- MR2 accepting an application profile inside a v3 checkpoint: CAUGHT by TestCheckpointIdentityAndCachedEventGuardsFallBackToFullAudit.\n- MR3 letting ratify@1 ratify a fold activation: CAUGHT by TestCurrentStateCannotActivateAFoldButLegacyHistoryStillReplays.\n- MR4 extending the legacy bridge to state@2: CAUGHT by the same test.\n- MR5 making /v0/rebuild always report quiet: CAUGHT by the restored cold-audit test, which proves that restoration is real coverage and not decoration.\n\nREPLAY PARITY - re-run at this head and still exact. internal/workroom is untouched since 768582b1, so the fold is the same code I proved parity on, but I re-measured against the current log anyway: gs built from main 84805b04 and gs built from 0c05eca5 produce byte-identical actors, acts, artifacts, commitments, decisions (6110 of them), omitted_supersessions, opaque_kinds, provenance, reviews and statements. The only difference anywhere remains vocabulary.definitions at 13 against 12, fold-activation leaving the starter catalog, which is the intended change.\n\nARCHITECTURE - pass. Layers affected: Git storage untouched; kernel (internal/kernel) loses the vestigial option fields only; application (internal/workroom unchanged since the reviewed round-one head, internal/app for the flight lifecycle); surfaces (internal/service tests, cmd/gs unchanged since round one). The delta changes no documented contract: it deletes dead fields, adds coverage, and closes a concurrency window, so docs/reference/architecture.md still matches - its internal/kernel row (a checkpoint caches only kernel-verified events and kernel identity, never projection state or an application profile) and its internal/app row (reuses the profile-independent authenticated prefix across fold changes and gates its separate projection cache on the selected application and fold version) both remain exactly true, and are now truer since the Profile alias that could have re-enabled the cache is gone. The round-one architecture conclusion stands unchanged: fold-activation leaves the current vocabulary, state@2 naming it is undefined-kind, the bridge replays state@0/state@1 history, and fold upgrades are initializing-key-signed host-binding replacements, which is existing code.\n\nSECURITY - pass. Fold-activation authority is unchanged since round one and re-verified by MR3 and MR4: state@2 cannot activate, ratify@1 cannot ratify an activation, the bridge admits only state@0/state@1, KindDefinition carries no code pointer and body.fold is refused. Untrusted inputs: the local checkpoint pointer is still validated as an object ID before reaching any Git command, manifest bounds are still checked before counts are trusted, and no new untrusted input is introduced by the delta.\n\nSECURITY, CHECKPOINT BOUNDARY - pass, distinct conclusion. checkpoint@3 identity remains kernel-only: schema, object format, genesis and authenticated sequencer-key lineage, with no application profile - and that is now structurally guaranteed rather than merely intended, because the option that could carry a profile no longer exists. A v3 carrying a profile field is still refused (MR2), and TestWrittenCheckpointContainsOnlyKernelVerifiedMaterial still holds the manifest closed-world at exactly six fields with none of profile, projection, vocabulary or fold. v1 and v2 compatibility is unchanged: both are accepted only after full authentication, each is refused when its historical profile field is missing, and that field is ignored rather than used as an eligibility key. Signature verification still precedes payload decompression, the marker that selects the schema branch is still inside the signed commit object, and marker and schema must still agree. Cross-repository substitution is still refused by genesis, object format and the requirement that stored events bind into the real named first-parent sequence. On completed-flight reuse across head and profile changes - the specific question this round adds - the answer is now correct at both layers: the kernel prefix is deliberately reusable across folds because it holds only verified event material, while the application projection is gated on application plus fold version, and the flight guard closes the one path by which a projection computed under the old gate could still be handed out after the gate moved. Removing the Profile alias also removes a latent trap I flagged last round, where a caller setting Profile would have got an enabled but unkeyed cache.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION - pass, no further changes requested. The delta is a net removal of compatibility machinery, which is what the split's own principle asks for. I looked specifically for remaining removable compatibility or concurrency machinery and found none worth cutting: Enabled is the minimum single switch, both test gates now have real callers, the historical bridge is still one ten-line function, and the flight repair adds a guard rather than a parallel path. The only thing I would even mention is that enabled() is now a one-line accessor over a bare bool used at six call sites; keeping it is defensible for uniformity and inlining it would be churn, so I am not requesting it.\n\nGATES at 0c05eca5 - go build ./... exit 0; go vet ./... exit 0; go test ./... -count=1 exit 0 across 25 packages with no failures; and the full race gate, go test -race -count=1 ./..., exit 0 across all 25 packages with no data races. That last one is the gate that exposed the completed-flight window at 73d4308c, so it is the one that matters here, and I ran it whole rather than on the affected packages alone. Head equals origin/request/schema-fold-split, the worktree is clean, the diff against the merge-base with main is still exactly the authorized 20 files, the delta against the round-one head is confined to 6, and the head is not an ancestor of main - no merge occurred.\n\nMERGE NOTE, not a condition of this verdict: internal/app now carries two live artifacts at that exact path, 1f73b755 and the child report 2e7f98e4. Paths match as exact strings, so the merge that lands this head must retire both at internal/app and publish one successor there, or the surviving one will sit live and silent afterwards. Citing the child here is what lets it close in the same merge, which is what the request intended.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:43430f1c993aa9db1995bb91cfb6845ad0db8801","head":"0c05eca5d7018f0c696c0e0df382e90649c9ccc6","verdict":"approved"}}